WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.800
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.480
Today, we are going to take a light simulation&nbsp;
algorithm that doesn’t work too well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:19.200
and infuse it with a powerful neural network.&nbsp;
And what happens, bam! This happens. Wow.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:26.720
So, what was all this about? Well, when we&nbsp;
fire up a classical light simulation program,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:36.240
first, we start out from…yes, absolutely nothing.&nbsp;
And, look, over time, as we simulate the path of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:43.840
more and more light rays, we get to know something&nbsp;
about the scene. And slowly, over time, the image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:50.960
cleans up. But there is a problem.&nbsp;
What is the problem? Well, look.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.520 --> 00:01:00.000
The image indeed cleans up over time, but no one&nbsp;
said that this process would be quick. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.280
this can take from minutes, in the case of this&nbsp;
scene, to get this, even days for this scene.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.560
In our earlier paper, we rendered&nbsp;
this beautiful, but otherwise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.240
sinfully difficult scene and it took approximately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:22.960
three weeks to finish. And it also took&nbsp;
several computers running at the same time.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.640
However, these days, neural network-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:33.280
learning methods are already capable of&nbsp;
doing light transport. Why not use them?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:40.400
Well, yes they are, but they are not perfect.&nbsp;
And we don’t want an imperfect result.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:47.520
So, scientists at NVIDIA said that this&nbsp;
might be the perfect opportunity to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:54.240
control variates. What are those and&nbsp;
why is this the perfect opportunity?

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:01.360
Control variates are a way to inject our knowledge&nbsp;
of a scene into the light transport algorithm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:07.680
And here is the key. Any knowledge is&nbsp;
useful, as long as it gives us a headstart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:14.400
even if this knowledge is imperfect. Here&nbsp;
is an illustration of that. What we do is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.000
that we start out using that knowledge,&nbsp;
and make up for the differences over time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:28.800
Okay, so, how much of a headstart can we get with&nbsp;
this? Normally, we start out from a black image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:36.240
and then, a very noisy image. Now hold on to your&nbsp;
papers, and let’s see what this can do to help us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:46.560
Wow. Look at that. This is incredible! This is not&nbsp;
the final rendering, this is what the algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:54.000
knows, and instead of the blackness, we can&nbsp;
start out from this. Goodness, it turns out that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:02.080
this might not have been an illustration, but the&nbsp;
actual knowledge the AI has of the scene. So cool!

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:09.680
Let’s look at another example. In this bedroom,&nbsp;
this will be our starting point. My goodness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:17.120
is that really possible? The bed and the&nbsp;
floor are almost completely done. The curtain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:22.240
and the refractive objects are noisy, but&nbsp;
do not worry about those for a second.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:29.120
This is just a starting point, and it is still&nbsp;
way better than starting out from a black image.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:36.320
To be able to visualize what the algorithm has&nbsp;
learned with a little more finesse, we can also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:43.440
pick a point in space, and learn what the world&nbsp;
looks like from this point and how much light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:51.280
scatters around it. And we can even visualize&nbsp;
what happens when we move this point around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.320
Man, this technique has a ton&nbsp;
of knowledge about these scenes.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:06.400
So, once again, just to make sure. We start out&nbsp;
not from a black image, but from a learned image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:10.720
and now, we don’t have to compute&nbsp;
all the light transport in the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:17.920
we just need to correct the differences.&nbsp;
This is so much faster! But actually, is it?

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:26.640
Let’s look at how this helps us in practice. And&nbsp;
that means, of course, equal time comparisons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:32.640
against previous light transport simulation&nbsp;
techniques. And, when it comes to comparisons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:41.600
you know what I want. Yes! I want Eric Veach’s&nbsp;
legendary scene. See? This is an insane scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:49.360
where all the light is coming from not here, but&nbsp;
the neighboring room through a door that is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:56.240
slightly ajar. And, the path tracer, the reference&nbsp;
light transport simulation technique behaves as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:04.000
expected. Yes, we get a very noisy image because&nbsp;
very few of the simulated rays make it to the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:10.960
room. Thus, most of our computation is going&nbsp;
to waste. This is why we get this noisy image.&nbsp;

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:17.360
And, let’s have a look what the new method&nbsp;
can do in the same amount of time. Wow!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:24.320
Are you seeing what I am seeing? This is&nbsp;
completely out of this world. My goodness.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:30.560
Okay, but this was a pathological scene&nbsp;
designed to challenge our light transport&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:37.920
simulation algorithms. What about a more typical&nbsp;
outdoors scene? Add tons of incoming light from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:47.200
every direction and my favorite, water caustics.&nbsp;
Do we get any advantages here? The path tracer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:53.200
is quite noisy, this will take quite a bit of&nbsp;
time to clean up. Whereas the new technique…oh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.320 --> 00:06:02.560
my, that is a clean image. How close is it to the&nbsp;
fully converged reference image? Well, you tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:09.520
me, because you are already looking at that. Yes,&nbsp;
now, we are flicking between the new technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:17.360
and the reference image, and I can barely tell the&nbsp;
difference. There are some, for instance, here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:23.040
but that seems to be about it. Can you tell the&nbsp;
difference? Let me know in the comments below.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:31.520
Now, let’s try a bathroom. Lots of shiny surfaces&nbsp;
and specular light transport. And the results are…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:39.600
look at that! There is no contest here. A&nbsp;
huge improvement across the whole scene.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:47.280
And, believe it or not, you still haven’t seen&nbsp;
the best results yet. Don’t believe it? Now, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:53.680
you have been holding on to your papers, squeeze&nbsp;
that paper, and look at the art room example here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.400 --> 00:07:02.560
This is an almost unusable image with classical&nbsp;
light transport. And, are you ready? Well, look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:10.720
this. What in the world! This is where I fell off&nbsp;
the chair when I was reading this paper. Absolute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:16.560
madness. Look. While the previous technique&nbsp;
is barely making a dent into the problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:23.760
the new method is already just a few fireflies&nbsp;
away from the reference. I can’t believe my eyes.

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:29.440
And this result is not just an&nbsp;
anomaly. We can try a kitchen scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:36.880
and draw similar conclusions. Let’s see.&nbsp;
Now we’re talking! I am out of words.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:44.320
Now, despite all these amazing results, of&nbsp;
course, not even this technique is perfect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:50.880
This torus was put inside a glass container, and&nbsp;
is a nightmare scenario for any kind of light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:56.240
transport simulation. The new method successfully&nbsp;
harnesses our knowledge about the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:03.120
and accelerates the process a great&nbsp;
deal, but, once again, we get fireflies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:10.080
These are going to be difficult to get rid of&nbsp;
and will still take a fair bit of time. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:16.640
my goodness, if this is supposed to be a&nbsp;
failure case…then yes, sign me up, right now!

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:21.360
Once again, the twist here is&nbsp;
not just to use control variates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:24.960
the initial knowledge thing,&nbsp;
because in and of itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:32.320
it is not new. I, like many others have been&nbsp;
experimenting with this method back in 2013,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:40.240
almost ten years ago, and back then, it was&nbsp;
nowhere near as good as this one. So, what is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:47.920
twist then? The twist is to use control variates,&nbsp;
and infuse them with a modern neural network.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.360
Infusing previous techniques with&nbsp;
powerful learning-based methods is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:59.520
fantastic area of research these days. For&nbsp;
instance, here you see an earlier result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:06.240
an ancient light transport technique called&nbsp;
radiosity. This is what it was capable of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:13.120
back in the day. And here is the neural&nbsp;
network-infused version. Way better. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:20.000
think this area of research shows a ton of promise&nbsp;
and am so excited to see more in this direction.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:25.920
So, what do you think? What would you use&nbsp;
this for? I’d love to hear your thoughts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:28.480
please let me know in the comments below.

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:34.720
And when watching all these beautiful results, if&nbsp;
you feel that this light transport thing is pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

